  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nMoisture will increase today with warm southerly flow out ahead of\nan approaching frontal system. Showers and possibly a few \nthunderstorms are expected today through Monday as the slow- \nmoving frontal boundary, and a supporting upper level trough \npush through. The heaviest rainfall concerns are going to be \nacross the southern Catskills and Poconos. Monticello in \nSullivan County has an impressive heavy rainfall forecast model sounding,\nespecially from midnight tonight through early Monday morning. \nPWATs exceeding 2\", short Corfidi vectors and a deep warm cloud\ndepth brings some cause for concern, especially since this area\nrecently saw heavy rains just a few days ago. \n\nPotential for localized flash flooding seems highest in this\narea overnight into early Monday. Instability also increases\novernight into Monday, with long skinny CAPE through a saturated\ncolumn, so with all these ingredients in place, there will be\npotential for intense rainfall rates and with the short Corfidi\nvectors, training of cells will be possible. However, some model\nguidance is suggesting the heaviest precipitation will also stay\nsouth of this area, so due to low confidence, decided to hold\noff on a flash flood watch for now. \n\nThe slow-moving cold front looks to exit the area by Monday \nafternoon and evening as winds shift to the northwest and drier \nair funnels in later in the day and into Monday night. High \npressure then builds in for a brief reprieve from the wet \nweather for Tuesday and possibly for some of the day on \nWednesday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nAfter brief high pressure, several features could come together\nto increase chances for showers and thunderstorms from late \nWednesday through the end of the work week. Moisture will\nincrease again across the region as the Bermuda high is \nforecast to shift west, drawing up warm and humid air from the \nsouth. Another broad upper trough over the northern Plains, \nUpper Midwest and Great Lakes will channel several short waves toward\nour region. Finally, a weakening cut off upper low over the \nTennessee Valley looks to gradually drift northeast across the \nsouthern and central Appalachians later in the week. The next \nfrontal boundary also looks to try to approach from the west in \nthe Thursday-Friday time frame and perhaps stall a bit. So \nputting everything together, there will be daily chances for \nshowers and storms with dew points anywhere from the mid 60s to \nas high as the mid 70s. This will lead to some very \nuncomfortable conditions with high temperatures mainly in the \nupper 70s to mid 80s each afternoon.",
